# .gitignore — rendered into a ticket-work repo by /setup (from templates/gitignore.tmpl).
# Edit the kit template, not this file, if you want the change to propagate to other repos.

# ── Ticket deliverables (CSV/TSV/XLSX/Parquet) ARE committed by default ──
# Results live with the ticket and show up in the PR for review. To keep a specific export OUT of git
# (customer data / PII), either name it `*.private.csv` (`.tsv`/`.xlsx`/`.parquet`) OR drop it in a
# `private/` subfolder of the ticket's final_deliverables — both are ignored at any depth (rules below).
# Deliverable SQL, QC SQL, and READMEs are always committed (reviewable artifacts).
#   Prefer the strict "no deliverable ever committed → docstore only" default? Uncomment these four:
#   # **/final_deliverables/*.csv
#   # **/final_deliverables/*.tsv
#   # **/final_deliverables/*.xlsx
#   # **/final_deliverables/*.parquet
**/*.private.csv
**/*.private.tsv
**/*.private.xlsx
**/*.private.parquet
**/final_deliverables/**/private/

# ── The Obsidian graph layer (tickets/graph/, tickets/objects/) is generated + committed by default,
# shared with the team like INDEX.md/OBJECTS.md. To keep it out of git, uncomment:
#   # tickets/graph/
#   # tickets/objects/

# ── Obsidian vault config. The graph-view config (.obsidian/graph.json — auto-written by the graph
# layer with the tickets↔objects filter + color groups) is committed so the graph looks right for the
# whole team; per-user session state is not.
.obsidian/workspace.json
.obsidian/workspace-*.json
.obsidian/workspace.json.bak
.obsidian/cache

# ── Source material: raw meeting transcripts stay OUT of git by default ──
# Curated excerpts are committed; raw full transcripts are not. The patterns below are ACTIVE, not
# a suggestion — a meeting transcript is the most PII-dense artifact a ticket folder holds. Note
# that the CSV-family rules above do NOT cover markdown, which is the format notetakers export.
# The committed, curated form is `YYYY-MM-DD-<slug>-meeting.md`: trim to decisions and action
# items, name it that way, and it commits normally.
# To track a raw file anyway: `git add -f <path>` plus an explicit approval. The kit's
# source_material_guard hook asks first either way, and it also catches a transcript whose
# filename gives nothing away (`notes.md`) — which no gitignore pattern can.
**/source_materials/*transcript*
**/source_materials/private/
# Downloaded attachments are inputs you didn't author and often carry PII — keep them local.
# Uncomment to ignore ALL of them, not just transcripts (you can still `git add -f` one you mean
# to track):
# **/source_materials/*
# !**/source_materials/.gitkeep

# ── Comms drafts (/ship writes draft-*.initial.md / draft-*.approved.md under a ticket's comms/) ──
# Unsent/approved wording is working state for the voice-refine diff, not a deliverable — never in a PR.
**/comms/

# ── Per-user / per-install (never shared); the team-shared .claude/settings.json IS committed ──
.claude/settings.local.json
.claude/worktrees/
# Which apps open your deliverables at a review gate is a personal choice, not a repo convention.
.claude/config/*.local.yaml
# The one-shot DB-write approval token (deny-with-escape on runtimes without an ask tier).
# Consumed on use and expiring in minutes — but never committable: a committed token would be
# a standing approval in the repo.
.claude/config/approve.once

# ── Python ──
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.venv/
venv/
.env

# ── OS / editor ──
.DS_Store
*.swp
*~
.idea/
*.iml
